    "text": "To determine the number of shirt-and-tie outfits that can be made, given that no shirt and tie can be the same color, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the total number of colors**: There are 8 colors available.\n\n2. **Understand the restriction**: We cannot wear a shirt and a tie of the same color. This means for each color of shirt, there are 7 remaining colors for the tie (since we exclude the same color).\n\n3. **Calculate the number of choices for shirts**: There are 8 colors of shirts available.\n\n4. **Calculate the number of choices for ties for each shirt color**: For each of the 8 colors of shirts, there are 7 colors of ties available (since ties must be a different color from the shirt).\n\n5. **Compute the total number of outfits**: Multiply the number of choices for shirts by the number of choices for ties for each shirt color.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of outfits} = 8 \\text{ (choices for shirts)} \\times 7 \\text{ (choices for ties)} = 56\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of shirt-and-tie outfits that can be made, ensuring that no shirt and tie are the same color, is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
